# Q5 K-mers are short DNA subsequences with length "k" bases, and are usually generated using the sliding window method we used in previous lecture exercises. Write a Python function that, given any DNA sequence, will print all the k-mers (e.g. 4-mers) that occur more than n times. E.g. with dna="ATGCATCATG", kmersize=2 and minfrequency=2 will output AT; because the kmers (kmersize) are 2 bases long, and there are three (minfrequency was 2) instances of "AT". Code you wrote for the previous lecture's exercises could be useful here...

def frequent_kmers(dna, kmersize, minfrequency):
    dna = dna.upper()
    kmers_found = []

    for start in range(len(dna) - kmersize + 1):
        kmer = dna[start:start + kmersize]
        kmers_found.append(kmer)

    for kmer in set(kmers_found):
        frequency = kmers_found.count(kmer)
        if frequency > minfrequency:
            print(kmer)


